            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "108"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.068,
            0.477,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari |Grade 9| Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 9:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of the circle is double the angle subtended by the arc at any point on the circle outside the arc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.202,
            0.415,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.145,
                0.202,
                0.415,
                0.392
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.165,
                0.401,
                0.398,
                0.434
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.21: Angle subtended by arc AFB"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.216,
            0.874,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " AFB is an arc. ∠ACB is the angle subtended by arc AFB at centre C. D is a point on the circle outside arc AFB (see Fig. 5.21)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.299,
            0.875,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We will assume for now that D is such that DC when extended cuts the circle at some point E on arc AFB, as shown."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.382,
            0.872,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We will consider the other case later."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.458,
            0.437,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To show:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BCA = 2 \\angle BDA"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.485,
            0.885,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is this true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Join D to C and extend DC to cut the circle at a point E on arc AFB. Now ∆DCB is isosceles with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "CB = CD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle\\mathrm{CBD}=\\angle\\mathrm{CDB}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.553,
            0.869,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "∠BCE is the exterior angle of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { B C D } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " By the exterior angle theorem,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.341,
            0.578,
            0.693,
            0.599
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_16_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle BCE = \\angle CBD + \\angle CDB = 2\\angle BDC."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.605,
            0.722,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, ∆ADC is an isosceles triangle with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CA}=\\mathrm{CD}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.633,
            0.192,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.631,
            0.501,
            0.651
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_16_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle CAD = \\angle CDA"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.659,
            0.871,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { A C E }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the exterior angle of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { A D C } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " By the exterior angle theorem,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.685,
            0.695,
            0.705
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_16_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle ACE = \\angle CAD + \\angle CDA = 2\\angle CDA."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.712,
            0.73,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BCA = \\angle BCE + \\angle ECA,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BDA = \\angle BDE + \\angle EDA"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.739,
            0.228,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.34,
            0.738,
            0.728,
            0.759
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_16_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle BCA = 2 ( \\angle BDC + \\angle CDA ) = 2 \\angle BDA."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.765,
            0.193,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.764,
            0.517,
            0.784
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_16_equation_21.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle BCA = 2\\angle BDA."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.792,
            0.887,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The explanation we have given does not work if the situation is as shown in Fig. 5.22, where the extension of DC meets the circle at a point E outside the arc AFB. We must rework the explanation for this case."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
